139
00:04:09,610 --> 00:04:12,840
So that's when I started, you know, looking at the open-source code other people had written.

140
00:04:12,840 --> 00:04:14,900
I was poring over that manual, trying things out one by one.

141
00:04:14,900 --> 00:04:16,399
What does a feature like this actually look like in practice?

142
00:04:16,399 --> 00:04:17,999
How exactly are these signals supposed to flow?

143
00:04:17,999 --> 00:04:19,359
From my perspective, all this stuff...

144
00:04:19,359 --> 00:04:22,440
Anyway, that was definitely the period where I grew the most.

145
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:28,560
So, for you, both before and now, your main focus has been on the M-series cores?

146
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:35,850
So, you've also worked with ARM's IP and SoC design for the MV7 and MV8 series, is that right?

147
00:04:35,850 --> 00:04:41,580
Right, my previous work was mainly with the Cortex-M series.

148
00:04:41,580 --> 00:04:43,420
And there's the R5 series, too.

149
00:04:43,420 --> 00:04:46,240
And on top of that, there's the A7, A8, and AE series. What about these ones now?

150
00:04:46,240 --> 00:04:49,779
So I've gone all-in on RISC-V.

151
00:04:49,779 --> 00:04:55,460
So, what do you think? How does RISC-V stack up against ARM right now?

152
00:04:55,460 --> 00:04:57,000
So, what are its advantages?

153
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:58,120
Here's what I'd say.

154
00:04:58,120 --> 00:05:00,660
First off, it's an open-source instruction set.

155
00:05:00,660 --> 00:05:02,320
As long as you follow the open-source license,

156
00:05:02,320 --> 00:05:04,130
you can take it and use it for your own commercial purposes.

157
00:05:04,130 --> 00:05:06,370
Another thing is, it's not like ARM.

158
00:05:06,370 --> 00:05:08,130
Because, back in the day, ARM was actually split into two different types.

159
00:05:08,130 --> 00:05:09,570
I'm not sure if you guys have ever looked into this,

160
00:05:09,570 --> 00:05:10,570
One of them is the public domain version.

161
00:05:10,570 --> 00:05:13,080
One type, the one with the complete instruction set, is more expensive.

162
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:15,279
But the development difficulty is pretty high.

163
00:05:15,279 --> 00:05:17,240
what it requires from your system

164
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:21,140
For an architect, there's another fundamental level where you can take those instruction sets and the features you want,

165
00:05:21,140 --> 00:05:23,659
You can build upon that foundation and expand it yourself.

166
00:05:23,659 --> 00:05:27,700
But in the end, the ownership, or rather, the right to define it, still belongs to a company like ARM.

167
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:29,300
Then there's the other type, the public domain one I just mentioned.

168
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:30,220
ARM has already developed it for you.

169
00:05:30,220 --> 00:05:31,520
You just take it and use it directly.

170
00:05:31,520 --> 00:05:32,380
But it's a black box.

171
00:05:32,380 --> 00:05:33,390
You can't see what's inside.

172
00:05:33,390 --> 00:05:36,950
So, for better companies, they tend to be more independent.

173
00:05:36,950 --> 00:05:37,830
Take Huawei, for example.

174
00:05:37,830 --> 00:05:40,390
What they're buying is the instruction set license.

175
00:05:40,390 --> 00:05:43,380
But they won't just go and buy the finished, high-performance chips that they make.

176
00:05:44,460 --> 00:05:46,540
But with RISC-V, the first thing is that it's open-source.

177
00:05:46,540 --> 00:05:47,770
So, like I was saying,

178
00:05:47,770 --> 00:05:50,930
You can build upon its extensions and instructions to create whatever you want.

179
00:05:50,930 --> 00:05:56,500
For example, let's say you find that the MMU or SMMU in RISC-V isn't good enough for your needs.

180
00:05:56,500 --> 00:05:57,960
or the PMU isn't good enough,

181
00:05:57,960 --> 00:06:01,430
You can build upon it and create your own version from scratch.

182
00:06:01,430 --> 00:06:03,570
Uh, and on top of that, you can mix and match them yourself.

183
00:06:03,570 --> 00:06:07,970
So you're completely unaffected by their, you know, commercial agreements. And secondly,

184
00:06:07,970 --> 00:06:10,940
And there are a ton of people doing this.

185
00:06:10,940 --> 00:06:11,700
So, what's the upside to all this?

186
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:13,460
So, for the past couple of years, I've been diving into RISC-V and all that stuff.

187
00:06:13,460 --> 00:06:15,740
It's kind of like Linux back in the day.

188
00:06:15,740 --> 00:06:18,120
A lot of people are jumping on board to play with it.

189
00:06:18,120 --> 00:06:21,360
So that's why its QEMU, QEMU is updated incredibly fast.

190
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:23,460
So, for your semiconductor company,

191
00:06:23,460 --> 00:06:26,660
So, when you're working on these extensions based on RISC-V,

192
00:06:26,660 --> 00:06:29,500
Maybe your design and architecture teams haven't even finished building that feature yet,

193
00:06:29,500 --> 00:06:31,500
They've only just proposed the feature and how it should be implemented.

194
00:06:31,500 --> 00:06:34,010
So you can use QEMU to get a head start on that.

195
00:06:34,010 --> 00:06:35,529
So you can start validating the software and everything.

196
00:06:35,529 --> 00:06:36,450
It's just so much better than...

197
00:06:36,450 --> 00:06:41,420
In any case, it's better than any other instruction set architecture you can find on the market right now.

